0000:                   ********************************************************************
0000:                   * krnp4 - User Register Dump System Call for NitrOS9 Level 2
0000:                   *
0000:                   * $Id$
0000:                   *
0000:                   * Copyright February,1989 by Ron Lammardo
0000:                   *
0000:                   * This system call can be used in an application program to dump the
0000:                   * current contents of all user registers in Hex,Decimal,Binary and Ascii 
0000:                   * (Registers "A" and "B" Only). This module MUST be present in the
0000:                   * bootfile. If no krnp5 module is found,change the "mname" and "nextname"
0000:                   * as appropriate.
0000:                   *
0000:                   * If there is a conflict with the code used for this system called, it can
0000:                   * be changed by resetting the equate at "F$RegDmp"
0000:                   *
0000:                   * NOTE: All registers EXCEPT 'CC' are preserved....NitrOS9 internally resets
0000:                   *       the condition code register upon service call exit.
0000:                   *
0000:                   * Edt/Rev  YYYY/MM/DD  Modified by
0000:                   * Comment
0000:                   * ------------------------------------------------------------------
0000:                   *   1      1989/02/??  Ron Lammardo
0000:                   * Started.
0000:                   *   2       2012/11/22 Gene Heskett
0000:                   * Remove final cr to save screen space
0000:                   
0000:                            nam   krnp4 
0000:                            ttl   User Register Dump System Call for NitrOS9 Level 2
0000:                   
0000:                            ifp1  
0000:                            use   defsfile
0000:                            endc  
0000:                   
00C1:                   tylg     set   Systm+Objct
0080:                   atrv     set   ReEnt+revision
0000:                   revision set   0
0001:                   edition  set   1
0000:                   
